<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Commune of the Working People of Estonia</h1><p class="st-meta">1918 CE – 1919 CE · capital Narva</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Soviet puppet government proclaimed in Narva in 1918, which tried to take Estonia for the Bolsheviks during the Estonian War of Independence.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>As German troops withdrew after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-11-november-1918">armistice</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> invaded <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-estonia">Estonia</a> and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narva">Narva</a> on 28 November 1918. The next day Estonian Bolsheviks proclaimed the Commune of the Working People of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> there, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaan-anvelt">Jaan Anvelt</a> as chairman, and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/russian-sfsr">Soviet Russia</a> recognised it in December.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Commune followed the Bolshevik model: it nationalised manors, factories and banks, and set up revolutionary tribunals that executed opponents, including clergy at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tartu">Tartu</a>. Its authority depended on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a>, which advanced to within about 30 kilometres of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/reval">Tallinn</a> by early January 1919.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Estonian counter-offensive, aided by Finnish volunteers and the British navy, drove the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> out of most of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-estonia">Estonia</a> by February 1919. The Commune retreated into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and dissolved in June 1919. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-tartu-russian-estonian">Treaty of Tartu</a> in 1920 recognised the independence of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/commune-of-the-working-people-of-estonia.jpg" alt="Commune of the Working People of Estonia" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Estonian Workers' Commune · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Estonian_Workers%27_Commune_(1918-1919).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1918 CE – 1919 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Narva</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Jaan Anvelt</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Estonian, Russian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/estonia">Estonia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Estonian Workers' Commune, Eesti Töörahva Kommuun</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1918&amp;focus=commune-of-the-working-people-of-estonia" aria-label="Commune of the Working People of Estonia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5274 183 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Commune of the Working People of Estonia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5594 439L5820 533M5594 439L5521 437"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5820 533h0M5521 437h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5594 439h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5594 439h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Narva. <a href="/atlas?year=1918&amp;focus=commune-of-the-working-people-of-estonia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

